  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 251 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory has been issued for Wednesday \n11 AM until 8 PM. Anomalously hot temperatures are expected to \npersist into Friday.\n\nThe Heat Advisory is in effect for most of the eastern Piedmont \nthrough the Sandhills and Coastal Plain. \n\nEven at this late hour (or early for some) - at 200 AM - it was \nstill 80+ degrees in the urban centers of the Triad, Triangle, and \nFayetteville. We will definitely be off and running when the sun \nrises. Expect mostly sunny skies and highs in the 97-100 range in \nmost areas. Heat index values will very near 100 in the NW and 105+ \nin the advisory area this afternoon. \n\nLows tonight will be in the mid to upper 70s. Again, it will be slow \nto fall below 90 this evening, and 80 at 300 AM Thursday.\n\nExpect additional Heat Advisories the rest of the work week.\n\nThere is a slight chance of PM thunderstorms this afternoon, mainly \nin the NE Coastal Plain, where some weak convergence with the \nsurface trough may work with MLCapes in excess of 2500 J/kg to force \na storm or two this afternoon. We do not expect anything more than \nisolated storms this afternoon. \n\nThere is a slight chance of PM storms again during peak heating \nThursday; however, the chance is essentially over and near the \nmountains with differential heating.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...Labor Day weekend through Monday gradually \ntrends cooler along with highest thunderstorm chances Saturday - \nshifting SE Sunday and Monday.\n\nThe chance of thunderstorms will go up somewhat Friday, mainly in \nthe north and east as some upper level energy over the mid-Atlantic \nstates may send outflows from VA convection into the region late in \nthe day. \n\nThe highest chance of thunderstorms appears to be Saturday as the \nbackdoor front and outflows work through the region during the \nafternoon and evening. It will remain hot and become unstable aiding \nin the low level forcing with the boundaries to work with. \n\nThe chance of showers/storms is expected to shift to the southeast \nand over the mountains Sunday and Monday as a cooler and more stable \nNE flow sets up. Highs in the 80s and lows in the 60s will be much \nmore tolerable for most!",
